  "title": "fix(sem): extract C and C++ data members",
  "body": "Problem:\nC and C++ struct/class data members were previously not extracted. The parser skipped all field_declaration nodes in these languages, leaving aggregates as opaque types with no searchable fields, no CONTAINS edges, and no targets for field reads. Additionally, tree-sitter-cpp misparses in-class data members with initializers (e.g., int total_ = 0;) as function definitions with pure-virtual clauses, causing them to be extracted as methods instead of fields, which then competed for receiver call resolution.\nSolution:\nRemoved the blanket skip of C/C++ field declarations and added language-specific extraction logic. A new cFamilyMemberDeclaratorName() function unwraps declarator chains (pointers, arrays, references) to find the actual field name in C and C++ member declarations, handling edge cases like function pointers (int (*handler)(int)). For C++, added cPlusPlusInitialisedMemberName() to detect and correctly classify data members with initializers by checking for a bare field_identifier with no function_declarator and a pure_virtual_clause, which distinguishes them from genuine methods.\nTest coverage added for all cases: C struct fields with various declarators, C++ class fields with and without initializers, and verification that methods are not misclassified as fields. The golden fixture shows two new field symbols (Entry.key in C++, Buffer.data in C) with corresponding CONTAINS and DEFINES relations, raising total symbols from 31 to 33, relations from 101 to 105, and field kind count from 20 to 22.",
